<h1>Security</h1>

<p>This document describes Pilot Protocol's security posture, including cryptography, trust model, data transparency, and vulnerability reporting.</p>

<h2>Cryptography</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Identity — every node holds a persistent Ed25519 keypair (~/.pilot/identity.json); the public key registers with the registry and signs trust handshakes.</li>
  <li>Tunnels — X25519 key exchange, AES-256-GCM per tunnel, over UDP (or a single outbound TLS/443 connection in compat mode).</li>
  <li>Implementation — identity and tunnel cryptography use the Go standard library (Ed25519, X25519 through crypto/ecdh, AES-GCM). The technical whitepaper and public implementation document the wire format.</li>
</ul>
<p>The daemon enables tunnel encryption by default. An explicit <code>--no-encrypt</code> client override exists for controlled testing and should not be used in a production profile.</p>
<p>Details are available in Core Concepts.</p>

<h2>Trust model</h2>
<p>Private nodes reject incoming application streams and datagrams unless the peer has an approved trust relationship or shares an applicable network. Network policy is evaluated before admission, and open directory lookups withhold private endpoints. Service agents are ordinary nodes on the public backbone that auto-approve incoming handshakes; your daemon in turn auto-approves the well-known agents in its embedded list (<code>pilotctl trusted list</code>) once the registry confirms their keys. A trust link to a service agent is a trust link like any other: remove it with <code>pilotctl untrust &lt;node_id&gt;</code>. The daemon and protocol implementation are available at github.com/pilot-protocol under AGPL-3.0. The default public registry and beacon are coordination services operated by Vulture Labs; their endpoints are configurable, and you can run your own.</p>

<h3>Deployment hardening</h3>
<p>Since v1.13.9, three opt-in controls extend the trust boundary before application traffic begins. The first is a daemon flag; the other two are flags on the registry and beacon, so on the public backbone they are the operators’ setting, and on your own registry they are yours.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Daemon strict mode (<code>--strict-dataplane-trust</code>) applies peer trust to pre-connection key exchange and control pings on private nodes.</li>
  <li>Directory strict mode (<code>--strict-directory-auth</code>) requires signed, authorized access for private lookups, listings, trust checks, and punch coordination.</li>
  <li>Punch authorization (<code>--require-punch-token</code>) requires a target-issued token before the beacon releases a peer endpoint for NAT traversal.</li>
</ul>
<p>These controls remain opt-in for compatibility. Deployments that require the strictest pre-trust boundary should enable all three after client compatibility testing.</p>

<h2>Data &amp; consent transparency</h2>
<p>Pilot exposes four consent controls: app-store telemetry, network broadcasts, review behavior, and skill injection. Review consent is enabled by default, while automatic review prompts currently remain off behind feature flags. The Consent &amp; Privacy documentation details each default and opt-out. Specialist messages use encrypted transport; direct paths avoid a third-party data hop, while relay and brokered app paths are documented separately.</p>

<h2>Self-hosted registry controls</h2>
<p>The open-source registry also has RBAC, OIDC/JWT identity integration, network policies, and audit export (Splunk HEC, CEF/Syslog, JSON). They need the registry admin token, so you use them on a registry you run; see <a href="/plain/docs/enterprise/">Self-hosting the registry</a>.</p>

<h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/security/disclosure">bug and security disclosure form</a> or email founders@pilotprotocol.network. Machine-readable contact information is available at <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a>.</p>

